Orbit-stabiliser and $abs(S_n)=2abs(A_n)$ give
$
  abs(ccl_(S_n)(gamma)) = 2 (abs(C_(A_n)(gamma)))/(abs(C_(S_n)(gamma))) abs(ccl_(A_n)(gamma)).
$
The subgroup $C_(A_n)(gamma)$ is the kernel of the sign homomorphism restricted to $C_(S_n)(gamma)$, so its index is $1$ or $2$. If an odd element centralises $gamma$, the index is $2$ and the two conjugacy classes have equal size, hence are equal. Otherwise the centralisers coincide and the $S_n$-class is twice the size of the $A_n$-class. Conjugating by any odd permutation supplies the second half.
